  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "<math> <mrow> <mfrac> <mrow> <mtext>d</mtext> <mi>θ</mi> </mrow> <mrow> <mtext>dt</mtext> </mrow> </mfrac> <mo>=</mo> <mfrac> <mrow> <mi>σ</mi> <mtext>A</mtext> </mrow> <mrow> <mtext>mc</mtext> </mrow> </mfrac> <mfenced close=\")\" open=\"(\" separators=\",\"> <mrow> <msup> <mtext>T</mtext> <mn>4</mn> </msup> <mo>-</mo> <msubsup> <mtext>T</mtext> <mn>0</mn> <mn>4</mn> </msubsup> </mrow> </mfenced> </mrow> </math>. If the liquids put in exactly similar calorimeters and identical surrounding then we can consider T<sub>0</sub> and A constant then <math> <mrow> <mfrac> <mrow> <mtext>d</mtext> <mi>θ</mi> </mrow> <mrow> <mtext>dt</mtext> </mrow> </mfrac> <mo>∝</mo> <mfrac> <mrow> <mfenced close=\")\" open=\"(\" separators=\",\"> <mrow> <msup> <mtext>T</mtext> <mn>4</mn> </msup> <mo>-</mo> <msubsup> <mtext>T</mtext> <mn>0</mn> <mn>4</mn> </msubsup> </mrow> </mfenced> </mrow> <mrow> <mtext>mc</mtext> </mrow> </mfrac> </mrow> </math><br /><br />If we consider that equal masses of liquid (m) are taken at the same temperature then <math> <mrow> <mfrac> <mrow> <mtext>d</mtext> <mi>θ</mi> </mrow> <mrow> <mtext>dt</mtext> </mrow> </mfrac> <mo>∝</mo> <mfrac> <mrow> <mn>1</mn> </mrow> <mrow> <mtext>c</mtext> </mrow> </mfrac> </mrow> </math><br /><br />So for same rate of cooling c should be equal which is not possible because liquids are of different nature. Again from equation (i)<br /><br /><math> <mrow> <mfrac> <mrow> <mtext>d</mtext> <mi>θ</mi> </mrow> <mrow> <mtext>dt</mtext> </mrow> </mfrac> <mo>∝</mo> <mfrac> <mrow> <mfenced close=\")\" open=\"(\" separators=\",\"> <mrow> <msup> <mtext>T</mtext> <mn>4</mn> </msup> <mo>-</mo> <msubsup> <mtext>T</mtext> <mn>0</mn> <mn>4</mn> </msubsup> </mrow> </mfenced> </mrow> <mrow> <mtext>mc</mtext> </mrow> </mfrac> <mo>⇒</mo> <mfrac> <mrow> <mtext>d</mtext> <mi>θ</mi> </mrow> <mrow> <mtext>dt</mtext> </mrow> </mfrac> <mo>∝</mo> <mfrac> <mrow> <mfenced close=\")\" open=\"(\" separators=\",\"> <mrow> <msup> <mtext>T</mtext> <mn>4</mn> </msup> <mo>-</mo> <msubsup> <mtext>T</mtext> <mn>0</mn> <mn>4</mn> </msubsup> </mrow> </mfenced> </mrow> <mrow> <mtext>V</mtext> <mi>ρ</mi> <mtext>c</mtext> </mrow> </mfrac> </mrow> </math><br /><br />Now if we consider that equal volume of liquid (V) are taken at the same temperature then <math> <mrow> <mfrac> <mrow> <mtext>d</mtext> <mi>θ</mi> </mrow> <mrow> <mtext>dt</mtext> </mrow> </mfrac> <mo>∝</mo> <mfrac> <mrow> <mn>1</mn> </mrow> <mrow> <mi>ρ</mi> <mtext>c</mtext> </mrow> </mfrac> </mrow> </math><br /><br />So for same rate of cooling multiplication of <math> <mrow> <mi>ρ</mi> <mo>×</mo> <mtext>c</mtext> </mrow> </math> for two liquid of different nature can be possible",
